#!/bin/sh
# mdev hook for the SD card (/dev/mmcblk0p1). mdev sets $ACTION to
# "add" (card inserted / coldplug) or "remove" (card pulled).
#
# Always lazy-unmount first: `umount -l` (MNT_DETACH) detaches the mount
# from the namespace immediately WITHOUT a synchronous flush, so it never
# blocks -- even when the backing card is already gone. A plain umount in
# that situation wedges in uninterruptible D-state on mmc I/O that never
# completes (Corgi's pxamci card-detect does not reliably signal removal),
# which then hangs poweroff's `umount -a` and leaves the machine
# semi-dead (found 2026-07-27). Re-mount only when a card is actually
# present (add), not on removal.
#
# SWAP. The card also carries this machine's only swap area, a 64 MiB file
# at /mnt/card/.zaurus/swap -- with 64 MiB of RAM and a ~68 MiB root jffs2
# there is nowhere else to put one. /usr/sbin/cardswap does the whole job
# (create, sign, swapon(2), swapoff(2)); see its source for why it is a
# binary and not four lines of shell here (this busybox has no mkswap,
# swapon or swapoff applet at all).
#
# THE WHOLE BODY RUNS IN THE BACKGROUND, as one sequence. Two separate
# facts force that shape, and the obvious alternatives were both tried and
# are both wrong.
#
# It cannot run synchronously. `cardswap on` writes 64 MiB to the card the
# first time a given card is used, which takes the better part of a minute
# (measured: 52s), and `cardswap off` calls swapoff(2), which pages
# everything still in swap back into RAM -- on a card that has just been
# physically pulled, I/O that cannot complete. mdev processes events one
# at a time, so blocking here stalls every other hotplug event, PCMCIA and
# wifi included, and in the swapoff case wedges mdev itself in exactly the
# D-state the umount comment above is about.
#
# But backgrounding only the cardswap calls, and leaving the umount in the
# foreground, is a race that loses -- found live on 2026-08-02. swapoff(2)
# takes a PATH, and `umount -l` had already detached the mount by the time
# the backgrounded `cardswap off` got to resolve it: /proc/swaps was left
# reporting the area as "/.zaurus/swap", a path that resolves to nothing,
# so the swap area survived a removal with no way left to address it. The
# ordering between the two is not incidental, so they belong in one
# process, in order.
#
# The cost is that the mount is no longer synchronous with the event.
# That is fine: mb-applet-card already polls for a card acquiring a mount
# and allows a grace period, precisely because mounting happens behind its
# back; and with no swap enabled -- the normal case for an insertion --
# `cardswap off` is a no-op that returns immediately.
#
# Poweroff is unaffected. /etc/inittab's `::shutdown:` lines lazy-unmount
# /mnt/card themselves before `umount -a -r`, so a teardown still in
# progress here cannot hang a shutdown.
#
# The orderly way to remove a card is still mb-applet-card's Eject, which
# turns swap off while the card is present and can therefore finish. This
# hook is the fallback for a card pulled without asking, where some loss
# is unavoidable whatever we do.
CARDSWAP=/usr/sbin/cardswap

{
	# Swap first, always, and before the umount: an enabled swapfile
	# pins the filesystem it lives on, and once the mount is gone the
	# swap area can no longer be named at all (see above).
	[ -x "$CARDSWAP" ] && "$CARDSWAP" off >/dev/null 2>&1

	umount -l /mnt/card 2>/dev/null

	if [ "$ACTION" != "remove" ]; then
		mount /mnt/card 2>/dev/null
		# Truncating rather than appending: /tmp is on the root
		# jffs2, so this log is flash wear. One insertion's worth
		# is enough to see why swap did not come up, and it never
		# grows.
		[ -x "$CARDSWAP" ] && "$CARDSWAP" on >/tmp/cardswap.log 2>&1
	fi
} &

exit 0
